--TEST--
`Core\Request\Part::filename` is the client's claim about a file on its own machine, so it reaches no path sink until `Core\IO::within` launders it — `rule:http-server/a-part-is-a-file-iff-it-carries-a-filename`, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`, `rule:http-server/a-path-is-never-derived-from-a-url`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The boundary this pins is the one PHP crossed: `move_uploaded_file` took a
// destination the application built out of `$_FILES['x']['name']`, and every
// upload traversal since has been that one line. Here the claimed name is a
// `tainted string` like any other byte off the connection, so a path built from
// it is refused where it is written -- and the refusal is the general one, not
// an upload rule, because `rule:http-server/a-path-is-never-derived-from-a-url`'s "a filesystem path is never derived
// from a URL at request time" is the same rule about the same bytes.
//
// The two readers beside it are not paths and are not refused: what a part
// declared about itself is readable, and it is only the sink that is closed.
foreach (Core\Request::files() as Core\Request\Part $part) {
    echo $part->name(), " ", $part->contentType(), "\n";
    echo Core\IO::read($part->filename());
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:14:24
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
